  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \nAn upper-level area of high pressure will remain over the\nSoutheastern United States today. Southerly flow should keep\nmonsoonal moisture streaming northward into Eastern Nevada today,\nresulting in isolated to scattered thunderstorms this afternoon\ninto this evening. A few of these thunderstorms could be strong.\nThe primary thunderstorm hazards will be erratic wind gusts near\n50 mph and dangerous cloud to ground lightning strikes. There is a\nmarginal risk (5-15%) of excessive rainfall in Southern White Pine\nand Eastern Nye Counties this afternoon into this evening. Heavy\nrainfall may result in flooding of recent burn scars. \n\nThe aforementioned anticyclone aloft will progressive retrograde\nwestward Sunday through Friday. As a result, the flow should shift\nfrom southerly to southwesterly, pushing monsoonal moisture\neastward. There is an elevated risk of wildfires in Humboldt\nCounty Monday afternoon into Monday evening due to southwest wind\ngusts near 25 mph and minimum relative humidity values as low as\n12 percent. A warming trend is expected Sunday through Thursday.\nBy Thursday afternoon, high temperatures across Northern and\nCentral Nevada will be two to four degrees above normal for this\ntime of year. High temperatures will remain above seasonal values\nFriday. Here are probabilities of maximum temperatures of 90\ndegrees or higher for select locations in Northern and Central\nNevada on Thursday, August 20th, 2026: \n\nBattle Mountain - 96%\nElko - 95%\nWinnemucca - 95%\nWest Wendover - 95%\nCarlin - 95%\nTonopah - 90%\nWells - 90%\nSpring Creek - 85%\nEureka - 85%\nEly - 70%\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High forecast confidence\nregarding the possibility of a few strong thunderstorms in Eastern\nNevada this afternoon into this evening. Moderate forecast\nconfidence in a marginal risk of excessive rainfall in Southern\nWhite Pine and Eastern Nye Counties this afternoon into this\nevening. Moderate forecast confidence regarding a warming trend\nSunday through Thursday. Low forecast confidence in elevated fire\nweather conditions in Humboldt County Monday. No deviations from\nthe NBM model output.",
